 Although the facilities were reasonably clean, Sarah was aghast at what she saw. The lack of tiles and adornments did not faze her. It was the showers. Hundreds of batteries of tiny shower cubicles, not unlike cages, stacked ten high, clung to the walls. Water fell from giant elongated shower-heads above the top tier only and then spilled down through the grates to the cubicles below. The top tier showers received clean water direct from recyc, but by the time it reached the bottom rung it was a cold opaque brown sludge of dirt and urine. Showering up top was by far the most attractive option, but the cost was so prohibitive most could only partake on special occasions. Before taking the ladder to the top rung - there was no other option - Sarah had to locate and block the massive floor waste drain on the main floor. It wasn\'92t too hard to follow the rivers of mire to where they merged and disappeared, but the drain was massive. Far too big to block off and even if she could the room would flood in minutes. She had not predicted the enormity of the facilities or the vast amounts of water circulating through the filtration system. She had envisaged blocking up the floor waste with a face washer, just as she had done as a child when she had made shallow, tepid little baths in the shower recess. 
