\'93Ouch, so much for wit.\'94 He said, faintly disappointment. \
\'93You\'92re much better at slap-stick.\'94 She said, casually reaching out and brushing Cracker\'92s disheveled dark locks to one side. The touch of her delicate, manicured fingertips was a tantalizing torment to Cracker, unleashing waves of warm pleasure and shrill pain. She would not love him; she had a real life and a real career ahead of her. He was beneath her. The thought that she would only ever see him as a hick-cousin or a retarded sibling was immediately confirmed when she fastened down a disorderly tress with a salivary thumb. \
\'93You don\'92t know me that well.\'94 He said, smiling and gently taking the offending hand and looking deeply into her eyes searching for the smallest hint of interest, but there was nothing. She seemed completely neutral and, in fact, more concerned with gluing down his disobedient fringe. Still smiling, he returned the hand to its owner who immediately licked it and recommenced sliming his hair.\
\'93No, no. I must protest,\'94 he said, smile gone. \'93On behalf of every boy who has gone to school with his mother\'92s spit in his hair, for every child who discovered the price of Christmas is having to kiss mustachioed aunts and goateed grandmothers. For kids who have endured endless uncomfortable afternoons on the lap of bachelor uncles or imbibed the special odour of stale cigarette and beer fumes produced in the arms of dear old granddad, I protest.\'94 \
Not knowing what to say Sarah remained mute, mouth slightly agape. \
His voice now rose, slightly and with his chest puffed out, he continued the rant. \'93No-one, alive or dead has ever wanted someone to spit in their hair. Most consider it an insult. Don\'92t get me wrong, I have no objection to the exchange of bodily fluids. Just when it\'92s consensual.\'94 Unable to maintain the fa\'e7ade a sardonic smile snaked across his face. \
\'93I actually thought you were serious for a second.\'94 She said slapping him, lightly for a change. Then, after a moment of thought, she said, \'93You\'92ve been practicing that, haven\'92t you?\'94\
\'93You have no idea how long I have waited for the opportunity\'94\
\'93You\'92re a fruit-loop.\'94\
Changing topic quickly he said, \'93Thanks for saving me, by the way. Those G-String boys don\'92t usually take no for an answer.\'94 
\f5\i What\'92s a fruit-loop?
\f4\i0 \
\'93Consider it pay back for getting me out of the nightclub.\'94\
\'93Good, we\'92re even, then.\'94\
\'93No, not quite. You saved my life. I only saved your backside.\'94 Thinking this was hysterical; she set free a colossal unladylike belly laugh and slapped Cracker on his right butt cheek, a bright but fleeting glint of desire in her eyes. Cracker didn\'92t notice, though, he was too busy rubbing his stinging rump. \
\'93Trust me, you saved my life. I would rather die than be raped. You don\'92t owe me a thing.\'94 He said, profoundly grateful he did not go 12 rounds with the bathroom bowel bandits.\
\'93Well, I think I at least owe you a drink. I boorishly dropped the last ale you gave me. One second I had a drink and the next my hair was on fire!\'94 \
\'93How clumsy of you.\'94\
\'93Exactly. After that embarrassing faux-pas, I feel obliged to buy you a drink.\'94 Sarah said enjoying the silly banter far more than any dialogue with colleagues or friends. Her mind drifted the distinction between colleagues and friends, she acknowledged, was rather vague as she never socialised outside of business circles. She was unable to nail down one conversation she had had in adulthood that did not involve commerce. 
\f5\i That\'92s all they ever think about, making money.
\f4\i0  This mere thought, in and of itself, was seditious and Sarah immediately purged it from her mind. 
\f5\i Where had that come from? Money is everything, remember? 
